## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly construction of a data pipeline with common tasks: deduplication, validation, timestamp handling, and partitioned Parquet, plus testing and monitoring.

## Key Concepts
- JSON Lines parsing
- Idempotent deduplication
- Data quality checks (required fields, types)
- Timestamp normalization to UTC
- Parquet writing and date partitioning
- Basic testing and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const readline = require('readline');
(async () => {
  const rl = readline.createInterface({ input: fs.createReadStream('logs.jsonl'), crlfDelay: Infinity });
  const seen = new Set();
  for await (const line of rl) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(line);
    if (!obj.user_id || !obj.timestamp || !obj.path || !obj.status) continue;
    const key = `${obj.timestamp}|${obj.user_id}|${obj.path}`;
    if (seen.has(key)) continue;
    seen.add(key);
    // normalization and persistence would occur here
  }
})();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for multi-region logs?
- What metrics and alerts would you add for production reliability?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical data quality, late data handling, and observability in a real-world lakehouse pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Per-record contracts and simple validator logic
- Upserts into Iceberg star schema with deduplication by event_id
- Late data handling with watermarking (15 minutes)
- Lightweight observability: lineage, conforming vs non-conforming counts, metrics sink
- Trade-offs: Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink for beginner workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator
function validate(record) {
  if (!record.event_id || !record.ts || !record.device_id) return false;
  if (typeof record.metrics?.value !== 'number') return false;
  return true;
}
```

